 the same job_id/spec; close_terminal_job and record_inventory can produce separately bound evidence for both. Each alias can then register and execute ordinals 0 and 1. An in-memory execution of the actual registration/validation functions accepted four distinct postrun keys for one identical job_id. Impact: restart/rebinding can repeat both queries despite the exactly-two claim. Minimal repair: enforce an immutable canonical job-to-event binding and uniqueness of postrun ordinals across that exact job identity, including existing aliases; test different event IDs and deadlines without modifying original receipts.","tools/r6_budget.py reserve(), tools/r6_recovery.py Journal.intent(), tools/r6_postrun.py postrun_handoff(), and tools/r6_batch.py model_handoff(): observed overruns block some new reservations but do not reliably block future dispatch. Existing Journal.intent replays return without validating the current budget; both handoff launch paths check STOP but not current budget admissibility. Thus an unlaunched event reserved before another event exceeds a cap can still launch afterward. Separately, reserve()'s identical-replay branch checks only totals(), skipping protected-capacity checks: an in-memory fixture reserved one development model session, observed 25 sessions against the protected development allowance of 24, and successfully replayed the reservation. Impact: previously registered work can proceed after actual excess, including encroachment on confirmation/review protection. Minimal repair: centralize current-ledger hard/protected-limit validation and apply it before every first dispatch and relevant replay; retain observation/receipt consumption and owned cleanup when blocked. Add regressions for overrun between intent and dispatch and protected-limit breaches below the total cap.","tools/r6_postrun.py postrun_handoff(): its recovery loop has no controller-enforced deadline, and CANCEL_OWNED is handled identically to an ordinary wait. The 15-second query and cleanup limits run only inside the worker. If that worker is suspended or stuck before reaching its timeout handling, owns() remains true indefinitely; STOP also cannot make progress. An in-memory controller probe returned CANCEL_OWNED for 100 consecutive observations and encountered only sleeps, with no escalation or deadline check. Impact: the controller can hold its writer lock forever and leave an owned query unresolved across restart. Minimal repair: persist an immutable dispatch deadline, enforce it independently in the controller, and use bounded identity-checked cancellation/reconciliation for worker and recorded child ownership; preserve ambiguity and never retry the query. Test a non-progressing live worker, STOP, and restart past the original deadline."],"schema_version":"r6-recovery/1","status":"CPU_CONTROL_FIX_REVIEW_RESERVED"}
